CP(1)                            User Commands                           CP(1)

NAME
       cp - copy files and directories

SYNOPSIS
       cp [OPTION]... [-T] SOURCE DEST
       cp [OPTION]... SOURCE... DIRECTORY
       cp [OPTION]... -t DIRECTORY SOURCE...

DESCRIPTION
       Copy SOURCE to DEST, or multiple SOURCE(s) to DIRECTORY.

OPTIONS
       -a, --archive
              same as -dR --preserve=all

       --attributes-only
              don't copy the file data, just the attributes

       --backup[=CONTROL]
              make a backup of each existing destination file

       -b     like --backup but does not accept an argument

       --copy-contents
              copy contents of special files when recursive

       -d     same as --no-dereference --preserve=links

       --debug
              explain how a file is copied (implies -v)

       -f, --force
              if an existing destination file cannot be opened, remove it
              and try again

       -i, --interactive
              prompt before overwrite (overrides a previous -n option)

       -H     follow command-line symbolic links in SOURCE

       -l, --link
              hard link files instead of copying

       -L, --dereference
              always follow symbolic links in SOURCE

       -n, --no-clobber
              do not overwrite an existing file

       -P, --no-dereference
              never follow symbolic links in SOURCE

       -p     same as --preserve=mode,ownership,timestamps

       --preserve[=ATTR_LIST]
              preserve  the  specified  attributes:  mode,  ownership,
              timestamps, links, context, xattr, all.  The xattr value
              copies the file's extended attributes (see the EXTENDED
              ATTRIBUTES section below); all includes xattr.

       --no-preserve=ATTR_LIST
              don't preserve the specified attributes (e.g. xattr to
              skip extended attributes that -a would otherwise copy)

       --parents
              use full source file name under DIRECTORY

       -R, -r, --recursive
              copy directories recursively

       --remove-destination
              remove each existing destination file before opening

       --strip-trailing-slashes
              remove trailing slashes from each SOURCE argument

       -s, --symbolic-link
              make symbolic links instead of copying

       -S, --suffix=SUFFIX
              override the usual backup suffix

       -t, --target-directory=DIRECTORY
              copy all SOURCE arguments into DIRECTORY

       -T, --no-target-directory
              treat DEST as a normal file

       --update[=UPDATE]
              control  which  existing  files are updated; UPDATE={all,
              older, none}

       -u     equivalent to --update[=older]

       -v, --verbose
              explain what is being done

       -x, --one-file-system
              stay on this file system

       --help display this help and exit

       --version
              output version information and exit

   Backup CONTROL values:
       numbered, t
              make numbered backups

       existing, nil
              numbered if numbered backups exist, simple otherwise

       simple, never
              always make simple backups

       none, off
              never make backups

EXTENDED ATTRIBUTES
       cp can copy a file's extended attributes (xattrs, e.g. user.*
       namespace attributes) from SOURCE to DEST.  This happens when
       any of the following is given:

              -a, --archive          (implies --preserve=all)
              --preserve=xattr
              --preserve=all

       Each attribute's name and value is read from the source and
       written to the destination after the file data is copied.  If
       the destination filesystem does not support extended attributes
       the attributes are silently skipped; if a particular attribute
       cannot be set, a warning is printed but the copy still succeeds.
       Use --no-preserve=xattr to suppress copying xattrs that -a would
       otherwise carry over.

EXAMPLES
       cp -a file.txt copy.txt
              copy file.txt to copy.txt preserving mode, ownership,
              timestamps and extended attributes.

       cp --preserve=xattr a b
              copy a to b, preserving only its extended attributes.

EXIT STATUS
       0      if OK

       1      if errors occurred

